The Windows libraries of SQLite, Lua, zlib, stb and miniaudio are built with MinGW-w64, and
have parts of its runtime in them: Copyright (c) 2009 - 2013 by the mingw-w64 project, under
the Zope Public License 2.1 and others (vendor/LICENSE-mingw-w64-runtime).

PHI's lib/phi/lang/float.phi (Float.Log, Pow, Atan2 ...) is written from fdlibm 5.3, with
corrections from FreeBSD's msun: Copyright (C) 1993 by Sun Microsystems, Inc. "Permission to
use, copy, modify, and distribute this software is freely granted, provided that this notice
is preserved." (The notice is in the file.)

A release's phi is one file with the .NET runtime inside it: Copyright (c) .NET Foundation
and Contributors, under the MIT licence, with the notices of what it's built from
(licenses/dotnet-LICENSE.txt and licenses/dotnet-ThirdPartyNotices.txt in a release).

PHI's repository has what PHI needs to call each (written from its headers) and its
licence; the libraries themselves are fetched from their makers by vendor/fetch.py, and a
release has them in place.
